==English==

===Etymology===
From earlier {{term|gugaw}}, {{term|gygaw}}, from {{etyl|enm}} {{term|givegove||gewgaw, trifle|lang=enm}}, a reduplication of {{etyl|enm}} {{term|give|lang=enm}}, {{term|geove||gift|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|giefu|lang=ang}}, {{term|geofu|lang=ang}}, {{term|geafu||gift|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|gibō||gabō|gift|lang=en}}. Compare {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|gyligjöf||showy gifts, gewgaw|lang=is}}. More at {{l|en|give}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɡjuːɡɔː/}}
* {{audio|en-us-gewgaw.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[showy]] [[trifle]], a [[toy]]; a showy [[ornament]] or [[decoration]].
#* '''1883''', [[w:Nathaniel_Hawthorne|Nathaniel Hawthorne]], ''Doctor Grimshawe's Secret'', ch. 15,
#*: It was a Saxon ornament. . . . Some Puritan, before his departure, may have thought himself doing God service by filching the old golden '''gewgaw'''.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# [[showy|Showy]]; [[unreal]]; [[pretentious]].
#* '''1678''', [[w:John Dryden|Dryden, John]], ''[[w:All for Love (play)|All for Love]]'', Scene II,
#*: The rattle of a globe to play withal,
#*: This '''gewgaw''' world, and put him cheaply off;
#* '''1855''', [[w:Alfred_Tennyson|Alfred, Lord Tennyson]], ''Maud; A Monodrama, X'', stanza 1,
#*: Seeing his '''gewgaw''' castle shine,
#*: New as his title, built last year.

